<title>USA: U.S. April non-farm payrolls rose 142,000.</title>
<headline>U.S. April non-farm payrolls rose 142,000.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1997-05-02</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department seasonally adjusted jobs data.</p>
<p> In 1,000s--Change     April  March-(Prev)   Feb-(Prev)  Apr96</p>
<p>  in Nonfarm Payrolls   142    139    175    314   293     172</p>
<p> Jobless Rate (Pct)     4.9    5.2    5.2    5.3   5.3     5.5</p>
<p>Earnings, Hours of Private, Non-Farm Production workers:</p>
<p>    -		     April  March-(Prev)    Feb-(Prev)  Apr96</p>
<p>Earnings/Hour(dollars)  12.14  12.15  12.15  12.10  12.10  11.72  Avg Weekly Hours	34.6   34.9   34.8   34.9   34.9   34.3</p>
<p> Manufacturing Hours   42.2   42.1   42.1   41.9   41.9   41.5</p>
<p> -Overtime Hours	  5.0    4.9    4.9    4.7    4.7    4.6</p>
<p>Non-Farm Month-On-Month Payroll Changes by Industry:</p>
<p>    In 1,000s --	 April  March-(Prev)   Feb-(Prev)</p>
<p> Goods-Producing	  -57     -9    -12    109   114</p>
<p> -Construction	    -44    -25    -27    104   108</p>
<p> -Manufacturing	   -14     17     16	3     3</p>
<p> Service-Producing	199    148    187    205   179</p>
<p> -Retail		     32     53     43     18     6</p>
<p> -Services		   93     72    111     86    67</p>
<p> -Government		 32    -27    -19     33    43</p>
<p>Aggregate Weekly Hours Indexes, Seasonally Adj. (1982=100)</p>
<p>    -			 April    March---(Prev)   April96</p>
<p> Total Private	    139.6    140.7    140.5    135.2</p>
<p> -Goods-Producing	 112.4    112.7    112.7    110.0</p>
<p> --Manufacturing	  107.6    107.4    107.4    105.7</p>
<p> -Service-Producing     151.8    153.3    153.0    146.6</p>
<p>Note--The indexes show total aggregate hours of production or nonsupervisory workers on private nonfarm payrolls by industry.</p>
<p>Number of Workers by Industry, Seasonally Adj. (1,000s):</p>
<p>    -			 April    March---(Prev)  April96</p>
<p> Total Non-Farm	 121,577  121,435  121,450  118,922</p>
<p> Goods-Producing	 24,442   24,499   24,501   24,209</p>
<p> -Construction	    5,570    5,614    5,616    5,353</p>
<p> -Manufacturing	  18,302   18,316   18,315   18,283</p>
<p> --Durable Goods	 10,761   10,764   10,759   10,654</p>
<p> --Non-Durable Goods    7,541    7,552    7,556    7,629</p>
<p>Number of Workers by Industry, Seasonally Adj. (1,000s):</p>
<p>    -			 April    March---(Prev)  April96</p>
<p> Service-Producing     97,135   96,936   96,949   94,713</p>
<p> -Transp./Utilities     6,434    6,416    6,415    6,294</p>
<p> -Wholesale Trade	 6,710    6,709    6,708    6,550</p>
<p> -Retail Trade	   22,025   21,993   21,971   21,415</p>
<p>-Finance, Insurance,</p>
<p>  and Real Estate	 7,118    7,095    7,094    6,942</p>
<p> --Services		35,266   35,173   35,193   34,117</p>
<p> --Help Supply	    2,422    2,427    2,428    2,272</p>
<p> -Government	     19,582   19,550   19,568   19,395</p>
<p>Household Survey-Civilian Employment Data, Seasonally Adj.  (Number, 1,000s):  April    March---(Prev) February--(Prev)</p>
<p> Workforce	  136,098  136,319  136,319  135,634  135,634</p>
<p> -Employed	  129,384  129,175  129,175  128,430  128,430</p>
<p> -Unemployed	  6,714    7,144    7,144    7,205    7,205</p>
<p> --Job Leavers	  754	784	784	787	787</p>
<p>--Job Leavers as</p>
<p> Pct of Unemployed   11.2     11.0     11.0     10.9     10.9</p>
<p>Wall Street economists expected April non-farm payrolls to rise by 211,000 and a jobless rate of 5.2 percent. They also had forecast a 0.3 percent rise in April average hourly earnings and the workweek to average 34.7 hours.</p>
<p>The non-farm payroll data are based on a survey of employers, and the jobless rate and other civilian employment data are based on a survey of households.</p>
